<h2 id="what-is-first-reads">What Is Amazon First Reads, and Why It Matters for EPUB Readers</h2>
<p>Amazon's <strong>First Reads</strong> program is the company's monthly "early access" perk for Prime members. Every month, a team of Amazon editors hand-picks roughly eight unreleased Kindle e-books across genres — historical fiction, mystery, thriller, romance, children's picture books, and so on — plus one short read. Prime members can download one of those e-books free during the month, and most months also include a free short read as a second free pick. Non-Prime readers can buy the same titles at a steep discount, usually $1.99 instead of the $4.99 to $5.99 list price.</p>
<p>The September 2026 lineup follows the usual two-pick structure. Prime members can claim <strong>one full-length e-book plus the short read</strong> at no charge before either title goes on sale to the public. This is the same two-pick structure Amazon has used since the early 2020s, and it is one of the few ways to read newly-released Kindle titles at zero cost without resorting to library loans or Stuff Your Kindle Day giveaways.</p>
<p>What makes First Reads different from other monthly freebies — and why this article exists on a site that focuses on EPUB conversion — is that <strong>some of the September titles are DRM-free EPUBs by default</strong>. Amazon's January 2026 policy change lets publishers distribute their Kindle titles as <code>.epub</code> files you can download directly from your library, no conversion required. If the publisher opted in, your First Reads download arrives as a real EPUB that opens in Calibre, Kobo, Boox, PocketBook, or any other reader you prefer.</p>
<hr />

