<h2 id="how-to-claim">How to Claim Your Free First Reads Pick in September 2026</h2>
<p>The claim process is straightforward but has two pitfalls that catch first-time visitors:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Use the browser, not the Kindle app.</strong> The "Read for Free" button does not appear in the Kindle or Amazon Shopping apps on iOS or Android. Open <a href="https://www.amazon.com/firstreads" rel="nofollow noopener">amazon.com/firstreads</a> in Safari, Chrome, Firefox, or Edge on a desktop or laptop.</li>
<li><strong>Click the orange "Read for Free" button, not "Pre-order for $X.XX."</strong> The page often shows a "Pre-order" link next to the free button. Only the orange button under the "First Reads" banner triggers the free download; the pre-order link will charge your card when the book ships.</li>
</ol>
<p>After clicking "Read for Free," Amazon confirms the order with a "Thanks, [your name]!" summary, and the e-book auto-delivers to the Kindle linked to your Amazon account and to the Kindle Cloud Reader. From there, you can read it in the Kindle app, on a Kindle e-reader, or — if the publisher opted in to the DRM-free EPUB program — by downloading the file from your Amazon library to any device.</p>
<p>The catch on the EPUB angle is that <strong>not every publisher has opted in</strong>. The Amazon First Reads lineup is curated by editors and includes a mix of major publishers (HarperCollins, St. Martin's, Penguin Random House imprints) and self-published authors. As of mid-2026, the share of First Reads titles available as DRM-free EPUB downloads is around 35-50% — so you cannot guarantee your specific pick will arrive as an EPUB. The FAQ below explains how to check whether your specific pick is available as an EPUB before you commit to claiming it.</p>
<hr />

<h2 id="epub-angle">The EPUB Angle: Why This Is the Most Useful Free Book Program of 2026</h2>
<p>Since Amazon's January 2026 DRM-free EPUB rollout, the gap between "Kindle book" and "EPUB book" has narrowed significantly. Previously, even if you could claim a free Kindle book through First Reads or Kindle Unlimited, you were locked into Amazon's ecosystem — Kindle app, Kindle device, or one of the clunky Calibre DRM-removal plugins. Today, a meaningful slice of the Kindle Store is one click away from being a portable EPUB that you can read on a Kobo Libra, Boox Page, PocketBook Era, or any other reader that doesn't carry Amazon's logo.</p>
<p>The September First Reads lineup is a useful test case. If you have been thinking about <a href="/blog/why-authors-leaving-amazon-2026/">switching from Kindle to a non-Amazon e-reader</a> but worried about losing access to Kindle books you already paid for, the DRM-free EPUB download option means you can start claiming new reads in EPUB format without abandoning the Amazon ecosystem. Claim the First Reads pick, wait for the publisher's EPUB file to appear in your library, download it, side-load it to your Kobo or Boox, and read it on whichever device you prefer.</p>
<p>For authors, First Reads is also worth tracking. Being selected by the editors is a meaningful promotional slot — the books that appear here typically see a sales bump that lasts 6-10 weeks after the First Reads window closes. Several authors have publicly thanked the First Reads team on social media within days of their selection. If you are an indie author wondering whether to apply (your publisher can submit titles via the regular KDP dashboard), the September list offers a window into the genres Amazon is currently promoting: cozy seasonal romance, mid-list suspense, and children's picture books are over-represented compared to literary fiction.</p>
<p>For the broader reader, the takeaway is that <strong>First Reads in 2026 is genuinely worth five minutes of your time</strong> if you have an active Prime membership. The two free picks per month, combined with the existing Kindle Unlimited free-trial offers and the September Stuff Your Kindle Day romance event (typically scheduled around September 25 each year), means a Prime member can stack 4-6 free e-books in a single month without paying anything beyond the annual $139 Prime fee. The catch is that First Reads selections vary widely in quality — customer ratings on the September list range from 3.0 (Always Be My Pumpkin) to 5.0 (Watch Her Run, The Ferryman's Daughter) — so the editors' curation is a starting point, not a guarantee. Browse the editor notes for each title before you commit to your single free pick.</p>
<hr />

<h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<p>Below are the questions readers most commonly ask about Amazon First Reads, especially in 2026 with the new DRM-free EPUB download options.</p>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
<h3 itemprop="name">Do I need Amazon Prime to claim First Reads books?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<div itemprop="text">
<p>Yes. First Reads is a Prime member perk. Without Prime, you can buy the same titles at the discounted $1.99 price instead of the $4.99 to $5.99 list price, but you won't get them for free. Prime Student, Prime Video-only memberships, and Prime trial accounts all qualify for the free pick — the eligibility check is on the account, not the membership tier. If your Prime membership is set to renew mid-September, the claim still works; Amazon checks eligibility at the moment you click "Read for Free," not at the moment the book auto-delivers.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
<h3 itemprop="name">Can I download First Reads books as EPUB files?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<div itemprop="text">
<p>Only if the publisher opted into Amazon's DRM-free EPUB program that launched in January 2026. To check whether your specific pick is available as an EPUB, claim the free book first, then open your Amazon library on the desktop browser (not the Kindle app). If the title shows a "Download EPUB" button next to the "Read now" or "Send to device" options, the publisher opted in. If only "Send to Kindle" appears, the book is still DRM-locked. Roughly 35-50% of First Reads titles are currently available as EPUB, and the share is growing each month as more publishers enable the option.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
<h3 itemprop="name">When does the September 2026 First Reads selection expire?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<div itemprop="text">
<p>The free claim window closes on September 30, 2026 at 11:59 PM Pacific time. After that, the September selection is removed and replaced by the October pick on October 1. Books you already claimed remain in your Kindle library forever, even if you cancel Prime later. The non-Prime discounted $1.99 price also disappears at the end of the month; the books return to their $4.99 to $5.99 list prices for general sale on October 1.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

